# 🔥 9. String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer

This is another **very common interview question**.

| Feature | String | StringBuilder | StringBuffer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Mutable? | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| Can change content? | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Thread-safe? | Immutable | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| Speed | — | ⚡ Faster | Slower than Builder |
| Common use | Fixed text | Frequent changes | Multi-threaded changes |

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🔥 10. StringBuilder

`StringBuilder` is **mutable**.

Its content can be changed without creating a new String object for every change.

```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");

sb.append(" World");

System.out.println(sb);
```

Output:

```text
Hello World
```

### Use when:

> You frequently modify text in **single-threaded code**.

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **Builder → Fast ⚡**

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🔥 11. StringBuffer

`StringBuffer` is also **mutable**.

But it is **thread-safe** because its methods are synchronized.

```java
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Hello");

sb.append(" World");
```

### Use when:

> Multiple threads may access and modify the same buffer and synchronization is needed.

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **Buffer → Safe 🔐**

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

